---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "Best Electric Pressure Washers", url: "/best-electric-pressure-washer/" },
    { title: "Water Supply Requirements", url: "/pressure-washer-water-supply-requirements/" },
    { title: "Not Building Pressure?", url: "/pressure-washer-not-building-pressure/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-06", modified: "2026-09-06" });
const title = "How to Winterize a Pressure Washer";
const description =
  "A cracked pump is a terminal failure on most electric machines. The ten-minute autumn routine that prevents the most common way these washers die.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "How to Winterize",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-to-winterize-a-pressure-washer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to winterize an electric pressure washer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. There is no engine to prepare, which is why people skip it, but the pump is full of water and water expands when it freezes. A cracked pump housing is a terminal failure on most consumer machines because the pump is not economically replaceable, and the motor being fine does not help.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What happens if a pressure washer freezes?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Water trapped inside the pump expands and cracks the housing. That is the single most common reason a machine that worked in October does nothing in April. It typically shows up as a leak or a complete failure to build pressure, and it is not usually covered by warranty.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is pump saver and do I need it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is a combined antifreeze and lubricant made for pressure washer pumps, sold as an aerosol or a bottle that fits the water inlet. It displaces remaining water and stops the seals drying out over months of storage. If the machine is stored anywhere that can freeze, it is essential.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use automotive antifreeze instead?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Use the product made for pressure washer pumps. Automotive antifreeze is not formulated for these seals and does not provide the lubrication that stops them hardening in storage. Pump saver is inexpensive and it is protecting a component that is not replaceable.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is one cold night really enough to crack a pump?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. It does not take a hard winter or a prolonged freeze, which catches out owners in mild climates who assume an unheated garage is fine. If the space can drop below freezing even occasionally, either store the machine indoors or run pump antifreeze through it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I store the high-pressure hose over winter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Drain it fully, then hang it in large loops. It is stiff and takes a permanent set if coiled tightly or crushed under stored items, and a kinked hose can burst under pressure. Check it for splits and bulges before the first use in spring.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What do I need to do differently for a gas pressure washer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Everything above, plus fuel. Either run the tank dry or fill it and add stabiliser, then run the engine so treated fuel reaches the carburettor. A half-full untreated tank is the worst state to leave it in, and stale fuel is why most gas machines will not start in spring.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What should I check before the first use in spring?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Flush the pump saver out with clean water, rinse the inlet screen, inspect the power cord along its full length, check the high-pressure hose for splits, and test the GFCI outlet. Then connect water, open the spigot fully and purge air through the wand before switching on.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "how to winterize a pressure washer",
      "pressure washer pump antifreeze",
      "pressure washer winter storage",
      "tools & equipment",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Electric Pressure Washers",
    link: "/best-electric-pressure-washer/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Maintenance
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Field Tested
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          How to Winterize <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">a Pressure Washer</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Electric machines have no engine to winterize, which leads people to assume no winter preparation is needed. The pump still needs protecting, and freezing is the most common way these machines die.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#why-it-matters"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See the Routine
          </a>
          <a
            href="#faq"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the FAQ
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-it-matters" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why It Matters</a>
          <a href="#the-routine" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Routine</a>
          <a href="#pump-saver" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Using Pump Saver</a>
          <a href="#hoses-and-nozzles" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Hoses and Nozzles</a>
          <a href="#gas-differences" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Gas Machines</a>
          <a href="#spring" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Spring Startup</a>
          <a href="#year-round" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Year-Round Care</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          This is the single most common reason an electric pressure washer that worked fine in October does nothing in April. Water expands as it freezes, a consumer pump housing is not designed to survive that, and the pump is not economically replaceable.
        </p>

        <div id="why-it-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why a Freeze Ends the Machine
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water left inside the pump after the last use of the season is the problem. When it freezes it expands, and it does so inside a sealed aluminium or composite housing with no room to give.
          </p>

          <p>
            The result is a cracked pump. On most consumer electric machines that is a terminal failure, not a repair, because the pump is not sold as a serviceable part and replacing it costs close to the price of a new machine. The motor is usually still perfect, which makes it particularly frustrating.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A single overnight freeze is enough. It does not require a hard winter or a prolonged cold spell.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>An unheated garage in a mild climate is not safe, because one cold night does it.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The damage often is not discovered until spring, when the machine leaks or produces no pressure.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>It affects induction and universal machines identically; motor quality is irrelevant here.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The high-pressure hose and the wand can also split, though those are replaceable.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Freeze damage is almost never covered by warranty, since it is classified as user neglect.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Ten Minutes That Matter Most</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">This is the highest-value maintenance in the category.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Nothing else you do to an electric pressure washer protects it as much as this. Cleaning the inlet screen extends its life, avoiding bypass running extends its life, using the right cord extends its life. Winterizing is the one that decides whether there is a machine at all next spring, and it takes about ten minutes once a year.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="the-routine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Autumn Routine, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Do this after the last use of the season, before the first frost is forecast rather than after it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. Flush the Detergent System</h3>

          <p>
            Run plain water through the siphon for a minute with the black soap nozzle fitted, so it actually draws. Detergent left to dry in the injector over winter is the usual reason a soap system stops working, and it is easiest to prevent now.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. Shut Down in the Right Order</h3>

          <p>
            Switch the machine off, close the spigot, then squeeze the trigger to release the pressure trapped in the hose and wand. Disconnecting a pressurised hose is both hard on the fittings and a genuine way to get hurt.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. Drain Everything</h3>

          <p>
            Disconnect the garden hose and the high-pressure hose, and let both drain fully. Tip the machine to let water out of the inlet and outlet. Drain the wand and shake the water out of the nozzles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">4. Run Pump Antifreeze Through It</h3>

          <p>
            This is the step that actually protects the machine. With the water supply disconnected, draw pump saver antifreeze through the machine until it comes out of the wand or the outlet. It displaces the remaining water and lubricates the seals, which also stops them drying out and hardening over months of storage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5. Store It Above Freezing If You Can</h3>

          <p>
            A heated basement or conditioned space removes the risk entirely. An unheated garage in a cold climate does not, even if it rarely freezes hard, because a single overnight freeze is enough. If the machine must live somewhere cold, the antifreeze step is not optional.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pump-saver" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Pump Saver: What It Is and How to Use It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pump saver, sold as pump protector or pump antifreeze, is a combined antifreeze and lubricant made specifically for pressure washer pumps. It usually comes in an aerosol or a bottle with a fitting that screws onto the machine's water inlet.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Buy the product made for pressure washer pumps rather than automotive antifreeze, which is not formulated for these seals.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Attach it to the water inlet with the supply hose disconnected and the high-pressure hose off.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Run the machine very briefly, only until the product appears at the outlet. Seconds, not minutes.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Do not run the pump dry looking for it to emerge. If nothing appears quickly, stop and check the connection.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Some products are designed to be pushed through without running the motor at all, so read the can.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Leave it in the pump over winter and flush it out with clean water in spring before first use.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The spring flush matters and gets skipped. Run clean water through the machine before the first proper use so the product does not end up on whatever you are cleaning, and so the detergent system starts the season clear.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have no pump saver available and a freeze is imminent, draining as thoroughly as possible and getting the machine indoors is a reasonable emergency measure. It is not as good, because water clings inside the pump regardless of how carefully you tip it, but it is far better than nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hoses-and-nozzles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Hoses, Nozzles and Accessories
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pump gets the attention, and the accessories quietly degrade in storage in ways that are annoying rather than terminal.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Winter Risk</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to Do</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">High-pressure hose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Takes a permanent set; can split if frozen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drain, coil in large loops, hang up</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Garden hose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Splits if left full and frozen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drain fully and store coiled loosely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nozzles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Get lost; can hold water and corrode</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shake dry, keep the set together in a box</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Black soap nozzle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The one that goes missing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Store with the others; it has no substitute</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface cleaner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water in the swivel and jets</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drain, dry, store indoors</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Foam cannon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Detergent dries in the filter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rinse, empty, leave the bottle open to dry</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Power cord</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cracks from cold and handling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Inspect the full length before spring use</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Coil the high-pressure hose in large loops. It is stiff and takes a permanent set if coiled tightly or crushed under stored items, and a kinked hose can burst under pressure, which is both dangerous and an expensive replacement. Hang it in wide loops rather than piling it in a corner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store the wand and nozzles together in one place. Losing the black soap nozzle is remarkably common, and it is the one you cannot substitute for, since it is the only tip that lets the detergent siphon engage at all. Machines with on-board nozzle storage largely solve this; if yours does not, a small box on a shelf works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gas-differences" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. If Yours Is a Gas Machine
          </h2>

          <p>
            Everything above still applies, because gas machines have pumps too and they crack in exactly the same way. Gas adds a fuel routine on top, and that routine is the reason most gas pressure washers will not start in spring.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Either run the tank dry or fill it and add fuel stabiliser, then run the engine for a few minutes so treated fuel reaches the carburettor.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Half measures are the problem: a half-full untreated tank is the worst possible state to leave it in.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Change the engine oil at the end of the season rather than the start, so contaminated oil does not sit all winter.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Check or replace the air filter and note the spark plug condition while you are there.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Store it somewhere the pump will not freeze, exactly as with an electric machine.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Never store a machine with fuel in it inside a living space, and keep fuel away from any ignition source.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Two Failure Modes</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Gas machines fail to start. Electric machines fail to pump.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Gasoline degrades within months, and a carburettor left full of stale fuel over winter is the classic spring problem. Electric machines have no such issue, which is exactly why owners assume there is nothing to do and let the pump freeze instead. Different machine, same lesson: the autumn hour decides the spring.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Our comparison of <a href="/electric-vs-gas-pressure-washer/">electric versus gas machines</a> covers the ongoing maintenance difference in full, and it is a bigger factor in ownership satisfaction than most buyers expect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Bringing It Back in Spring
          </h2>

          <p>
            Five minutes of checks before the first job of the year, and they catch the things that cause a bad first session.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Inspect the full length of the power cord for nicks and cracks, and check the plug. Water and mains electricity in close proximity make this a genuine safety check rather than a formality.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Check the high-pressure hose for splits, bulges and kinks that set over winter.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Pull and rinse the inlet screen, which will have collected debris from the last session.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Flush pump saver out with clean water before the first proper use.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Connect the water, open the spigot fully, and purge air through the wand before switching the motor on.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Test the GFCI outlet before plugging in, because they fail without any indication that they have.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Run the machine briefly at low pressure and check for leaks at every fitting before starting real work.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            That purge step is worth doing properly every time, not just in spring. Starting a pump dry, even briefly, damages the seals, so connect the hose, open the spigot fully, squeeze the trigger until water flows steadily, and only then switch on. Our guide to <a href="/pressure-washer-water-supply-requirements/">water supply requirements</a> covers the whole startup sequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the machine has been stored properly and still will not build pressure, the cause is almost always on the water side rather than a frozen pump: a partly open spigot, a narrow or kinked hose, or a blocked inlet screen. Our <a href="/pressure-washer-not-building-pressure/">troubleshooting guide</a> works through it in order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="year-round" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Habits That Matter the Rest of the Year
          </h2>

          <p>
            Winterizing decides whether the machine survives. These decide how long it lasts once it has.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Habit</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Prevents</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water on before the motor, air purged</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seal damage from dry running</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Never running in bypass</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The leading cause of pump failure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5/8 or 3/4 in hose, 25 ft, spigot fully open</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cavitation from a starved pump</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rinsing the inlet screen a few times a season</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gradual pressure loss and grit in the pump</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12 AWG outdoor cord, kept short</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Voltage drop and motor overheating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flushing detergent through after use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A blocked injector and a dead soap system</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Releasing trapped pressure before disconnecting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fitting damage and injury</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The bypass line is worth repeating because it is the most common avoidable failure during the season. With the motor on and the trigger released, water circulates inside the pump and heats up quickly, and sustained bypass running kills pumps. A Total Stop System shuts the machine off automatically when you release the trigger, which is why it is a feature worth insisting on when buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of these take real time. Together they are close to the whole difference between a consumer pump that lasts a decade and one that fails in its third season, and they cost nothing but the habit. The picks in our <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/">electric pressure washer guide</a> note which machines have the features that make these habits automatic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Electric Means Nothing to Winterize</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                There is no engine, so people skip the whole routine. The pump is still full of water, and water expands as it freezes inside a housing with no room to give. On most consumer machines a cracked pump is terminal, because the pump is not economically replaceable and the motor being fine does not help.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting an Unheated Garage in a Mild Climate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single overnight freeze is enough to crack a pump. It does not need a hard winter or a sustained cold spell, which is exactly why people in mild regions get caught out. If the machine lives somewhere that can drop below freezing at all, pump antifreeze is not optional.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Pump Dry Hunting for the Antifreeze</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pump saver should appear at the outlet within seconds. Running the motor longer while nothing emerges means the pump is running dry, which damages exactly the seals you are trying to protect. If nothing appears quickly, stop and check the connection rather than persisting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coiling the High-Pressure Hose Tightly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is stiff and takes a permanent set if coiled tight or crushed under stored items, and a kinked high-pressure hose can burst under load. Hang it in wide loops rather than piling it in a corner, and check it for splits and bulges before the first use of the season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Spring Flush</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pump saver left in the machine ends up on whatever you clean first and can clog the detergent system. Run clean water through before the first proper job, and while you are there rinse the inlet screen and inspect the power cord along its full length for winter damage.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to winterize an electric pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. There is no engine to prepare, which is why people skip it, but the pump is full of water and water expands when it freezes. A cracked pump housing is a terminal failure on most consumer machines because the pump is not economically replaceable, and the motor being fine does not help.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if a pressure washer freezes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water trapped inside the pump expands and cracks the housing. That is the single most common reason a machine that worked in October does nothing in April. It typically shows up as a leak or a complete failure to build pressure, and it is not usually covered by warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is pump saver and do I need it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a combined antifreeze and lubricant made for pressure washer pumps, sold as an aerosol or a bottle that fits the water inlet. It displaces remaining water and stops the seals drying out over months of storage. If the machine is stored anywhere that can freeze, it is essential.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use automotive antifreeze instead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the product made for pressure washer pumps. Automotive antifreeze is not formulated for these seals and does not provide the lubrication that stops them hardening in storage. Pump saver is inexpensive and it is protecting a component that is not replaceable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is one cold night really enough to crack a pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. It does not take a hard winter or a prolonged freeze, which catches out owners in mild climates who assume an unheated garage is fine. If the space can drop below freezing even occasionally, either store the machine indoors or run pump antifreeze through it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I store the high-pressure hose over winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Drain it fully, then hang it in large loops. It is stiff and takes a permanent set if coiled tightly or crushed under stored items, and a kinked hose can burst under pressure. Check it for splits and bulges before the first use in spring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do I need to do differently for a gas pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Everything above, plus fuel. Either run the tank dry or fill it and add stabiliser, then run the engine so treated fuel reaches the carburettor. A half-full untreated tank is the worst state to leave it in, and stale fuel is why most gas machines will not start in spring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I check before the first use in spring?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Flush the pump saver out with clean water, rinse the inlet screen, inspect the power cord along its full length, check the high-pressure hose for splits, and test the GFCI outlet. Then connect water, open the spigot fully and purge air through the wand before switching on.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

