            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Water left in the pump over a freeze cracks the housing, and on most consumer electric machines that ends the machine, because the pump is not economically replaceable. A single overnight freeze is enough, which is why an unheated garage in a mild climate is not the safe option people assume it is.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The routine takes ten minutes. Flush the detergent system, shut down in order and release the trapped pressure, drain the hoses and the machine, then draw pump saver antifreeze through until it appears at the outlet. Store it above freezing if you possibly can, and coil the high-pressure hose in large loops so it does not take a permanent set.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            In spring, flush the pump saver out with clean water, rinse the inlet screen, and inspect the power cord along its full length before plugging anything in. Then keep the habits that matter the rest of the year: water on before the motor, never leave it running in bypass, and feed it from a short wide hose on a fully open spigot.
          </p>
        </div>


